{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "def sort_third(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    result_list = [None] * len(l) # Initialize a new list to build the result.\n    \n    elements_at_divisible_indices = []\n    divisible_indices = []\n\n    # Iterate to identify elements for sorting and preserve others.\n    for i, item in enumerate(l):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            elements_at_divisible_indices.append(item)\n            divisible_indices.append(i)\n        else:\n            result_list[i] = item\n\n    sorted_elements = sorted(elements_at_divisible_indices) # Sort the extracted elements.\n\n    # Place the sorted elements back into the result list at their original divisible indices.\n    for i in range(len(sorted_elements)):\n        result_list[divisible_indices[i]] = sorted_elements[i]\n        \n    return result_list"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial xs[0] + xs[1]*x + xs[2]*x^2 + ... + xs[n]*x^n at given point x.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum(coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs))\n\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a single zero point x for a polynomial with coefficients xs such that poly(x) = 0, even if multiple zeros exist.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Critical requirement: \"Trust the caller's inputs\" implies that the input constraints\n    # (even number of coefficients, non-zero largest coefficient) are met by the caller.\n    # This guarantees an odd-degree polynomial, hence a real root exists.\n\n    # Special case for degree 1 polynomial (ax + b = 0)\n    if len(xs) == 2:\n        return -xs[0] / xs[1]\n\n    # Use Bisection Method for higher degree polynomials\n    low = -1_000_000.0\n    high = 1_000_000.0\n    tolerance = 1e-7\n    max_iterations = 100\n\n    # Bisection method requires f(low) and f(high) to have opposite signs.\n    # For an odd-degree polynomial and sufficiently wide bounds, this is guaranteed.\n    # If a boundary is already a root, return it.\n    if abs(poly(xs, low)) < tolerance:\n        return low\n    if abs(poly(xs, high)) < tolerance:\n        return high\n\n    for _ in range(max_iterations):\n        mid = (low + high) / 2.0\n        f_mid = poly(xs, mid)\n\n        if abs(f_mid) < tolerance:\n            return mid\n\n        f_low = poly(xs, low) # Re-evaluate f_low as 'low' might change\n\n        if f_low * f_mid < 0:\n            high = mid\n        else:\n            low = mid\n\n    return (low + high) / 2.0\n```"}
