{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "import math\n\n\ndef _validate_constraints(xs: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates coefficient constraints for zero-finding.\n    Checks for even number of coefficients and non-zero largest coefficient.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not xs:\n        return False\n    if len(xs) % 2 != 0:\n        return False\n    if xs[-1] == 0:\n        return False\n    return True\n\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    return xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[1] * x^2 + .... xs[n] * x^n\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\n\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    \"\"\"\n    # As per the design specification, _validate_constraints is called.\n    # Per \"Trust the caller's inputs\", we assume valid inputs are provided,\n    # so _validate_constraints would effectively return True.\n    _validate_constraints(xs)\n\n    # Bisection method for finding a root.\n    # For an odd-degree polynomial (guaranteed by input constraints), a real root always exists.\n    a, b = -1.0, 1.0\n    epsilon = 1e-7\n    max_iterations = 1000\n\n    # Quickly check if 0 is a root.\n    if abs(poly(xs, 0.0)) < epsilon:\n        return 0.0\n\n    # Expand search interval until poly(a) and poly(b) have opposite signs.\n    # This loop will terminate for valid odd-degree polynomial inputs.\n    while True:\n        pa = poly(xs, a)\n        pb = poly(xs, b)\n\n        if abs(pa) < epsilon: return a\n        if abs(pb) < epsilon: return b\n\n        if pa * pb < 0: # Signs are opposite, interval is bracketed.\n            break\n        \n        a *= 2.0\n        b *= 2.0\n\n    # Apply bisection method within the bracketing interval.\n    for _ in range(max_iterations):\n        mid = (a + b) / 2\n        p_mid = poly(xs, mid)\n\n        if abs(p_mid) < epsilon:\n            return mid\n\n        # Determine which half the root lies in.\n        if poly(xs, a) * p_mid < 0:\n            b = mid\n        else:\n            a = mid\n            \n    return (a + b) / 2"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "def _is_divisible_by_three(index: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if an index is divisible by three.\n    Returns True if the index is divisible by three, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return index % 3 == 0\n\ndef _extract_divisible_elements(input_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Extracts elements from input_list at indices divisible by three.\n    This function iterates through the list to collect specific elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    divisible_elements = []\n    for i in range(len(input_list)):\n        if _is_divisible_by_three(i):\n            divisible_elements.append(input_list[i])\n    return divisible_elements\n\ndef _sort_elements(elements: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Sorts a list of elements in ascending order.\n    Returns a new list with sorted elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sorted(elements)\n\ndef _reconstruct_list(original_list: list, sorted_elements: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Combines preserved and sorted elements into an output list.\n    Creates a new list, filling in sorted elements at divisible indices while preserving others.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list = list(original_list)\n    sorted_idx = 0\n    for i in range(len(result_list)):\n        if _is_divisible_by_three(i):\n            result_list[i] = sorted_elements[sorted_idx]\n            sorted_idx += 1\n    return result_list\n\n# _get_divisible_indices is not explicitly used by sort_third in the final flow,\n# as _extract_divisible_elements already handles index checking directly while extracting.\n# However, for completeness as per design, including it.\ndef _get_divisible_indices(list_length: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Gets all indices divisible by three for a given list length.\n    Returns a list of integer indices.\n    \"\"\"\n    return [i for i in range(list_length) if _is_divisible_by_three(i)]\n\ndef sort_third(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Element Extractor\n    divisible_elements = _extract_divisible_elements(l)\n\n    # Sorter\n    sorted_divisible_elements = _sort_elements(divisible_elements)\n\n    # List Reconstructor\n    result = _reconstruct_list(l, sorted_divisible_elements)\n\n    return result"}
